2
.
To ensure your application consistently receives mail after a reconnection, variable registration should happen inside OnConnectToServer(), which is triggered every time a connection is established with the MOOSDB
3
.
Conceptual Correction
The recommended pattern is to create a dedicated helper function, such as registerVariables(), and invoke it within OnConnectToServer(). This ensures that registrations are sent to the MOOSDB both during the initial launch and after any subsequent reconnection
4
.
void Odometry::registerVariables()
{
  // AppCastingMOOSApp users should also call the superclass method here
  // AppCastingMOOSApp::RegisterVariables(); 

  Register("NAV_X", 0);
  Register("NAV_Y", 0);
}

bool Odometry::OnStartUp()
{
  // Perform one-time initializations, such as reading .moos file parameters [1].
  return true;
}

bool Odometry::OnConnectToServer()
{
  // This is called every time the app establishes a connection [3].
  registerVariables();
  return true;
}

Why this is necessary:
Subscription Memory: When an app connects to the MOOSDB, it must register for the mail it needs
2
. The MOOSDB only stores the latest value for variables and does not "remember" what a previously connected instance of your app wanted
2
.
Reconnection Lifecycle: If your application loses its connection (e.g., due to network issues or a MOOSDB restart) and then reconnects, OnStartUp() will not be called again
1
. Only OnConnectToServer() is executed upon the new connection
3
4
.
Standard Practice: In the MOOS-IvP development environment, it is standard practice to wrap registrations in a registerVariables() function to be called in both OnStartUp() and OnConnectToServer(), or simply in OnConnectToServer() to handle the "fresh connection" state reliably
