chatgpt.text:
The fragile part is that registration is only happening in OnStartUp().

In MOOS, OnStartUp() runs when the app starts, but OnConnectToServer() runs whenever the app connects or reconnects to the MOOSDB. If the app disconnects and reconnects, old subscriptions may not be reliably restored unless you register again.

The corrected pattern is:

C++
bool Odometry::OnStartUp()
{
  RegisterVariables();
  return true;
}

bool Odometry::OnConnectToServer()
{
  RegisterVariables();
  return true;
}

void Odometry::RegisterVariables()
{
  Register("NAV_X", 0);
  Register("NAV_Y", 0);
}

Conceptually:

OnStartUp() is for initialization: reading config parameters, setting defaults, initializing member variables, and doing initial registration.

OnConnectToServer() is for connection-dependent setup: registering or re-registering for MOOS variables after the app connects to the MOOSDB.

So the rule is:

Every variable your app depends on should be registered in OnConnectToServer(), usually by calling a shared RegisterVariables() helper. It is also common to call that same helper from OnStartUp() so initial startup works cleanly.

For a pOdometry-style app, that means NAV_X and NAV_Y should be registered there, not only once at startup.

A more complete skeleton:

C++
bool Odometry::OnStartUp()
{
  // Read configuration params here if needed.
  // Example: m_total = 0;

  RegisterVariables();
  return true;
}

bool Odometry::OnConnectToServer()
{
  RegisterVariables();
  return true;
}

void Odometry::RegisterVariables()
{
  Register("NAV_X", 0);
  Register("NAV_Y", 0);
}

Also make sure RegisterVariables() is declared in the class header:

C++
void RegisterVariables();

The mental model is:

OnStartUp()
  initialize app state
  read .moos config
  maybe call RegisterVariables()

OnConnectToServer()
  register/re-register for MOOS mail

OnNewMail()
  consume incoming NAV_X / NAV_Y updates
  update cached state only

Iterate()
  do periodic computation/publication if appropriate

So if the symptom is “after reconnecting, I stop receiving NAV_X / NAV_Y,” the first thing to check is whether your subscriptions are being renewed in OnConnectToServer().

claude.text:
The Bug
The Bug

Registration is happening in OnStartUp(), but it should happen in OnConnectToServer().

Here's why: in the pOdometry / MOOS app lifecycle, OnStartUp() runs once at launch — before a stable server connection is guaranteed. If the MOOSDB isn't ready yet, the Register calls either silently fail or get lost. More critically, if the app ever disconnects and reconnects (network blip, MOOSDB restart, etc.), OnStartUp() is not called again — so your subscriptions are never re-established, and NAV_X/NAV_Y mail stops arriving.

OnConnectToServer(), by contrast, is called every time the app successfully connects — including on reconnection. That makes it the correct and idiomatic place to register for mail.

The Fix
cpp
bool Odometry::OnStartUp()
{
  // Read config, initialize state, etc.
  // Do NOT register here.
  return true;
}

bool Odometry::OnConnectToServer()
{
  Register("NAV_X", 0);
  Register("NAV_Y", 0);
  return true;
}
Why This Works
Event	OnStartUp()	OnConnectToServer()
Called at launch	✅	✅ (after startup)
Called on reconnect	❌	✅
Server connection guaranteed	❌	✅

The mental model: think of OnStartUp() as "initialize my own state" and OnConnectToServer() as "tell the community what I care about." Every time you join (or rejoin) the community, you re-announce your subscriptions.

gemini.text:
The issue you are running into is a classic lifecycle mismatch in publish-subscribe or messaging architectures (common in middleware like MOOS, ROS, or custom sockets).

By registering for NAV_X and NAV_Y inside OnStartUp(), you are telling the application what it cares about only once when the process boots. However, when the application loses its connection and reconnects, the server/broker usually wipes that client's session state. The server doesn't remember what you subscribed to before the drop, and since OnStartUp() doesn't run again, you are left in a state of silent starvation.

Here is how to correct the pattern conceptually and structurally.

The Corrected Pattern

Subscription registration should happen inside OnConnectToServer() (or your framework's equivalent connection-established callback). This ensures that every single time a fresh connection is minted, the server is immediately told what data this application expects to receive.

Here is how the code should look:

C++
bool Odometry::OnStartUp()
{
  // Perform one-time application initialization here
  // (e.g., reading configuration files, initializing local variables)
  return true;
}

bool Odometry::OnConnectToServer()
{
  // Register/Subscribe HERE. 
  // This executes on the initial connection AND any subsequent reconnections.
  Register("NAV_X", 0);
  Register("NAV_Y", 0);

  return true;
}

Why This Fixes the Issue

To look at it conceptually, consider the difference between the two lifecycles:

1. The Flawed Lifecycle (Current)

App Starts: OnStartUp() runs → Registers for NAV_X/NAV_Y.

App Connects: Server acknowledges the registration. Data flows.

Network Drops: Connection is lost.

App Reconnects: OnConnectToServer() triggers (does nothing). The server treats this as a blank-slate connection and sends no data.

2. The Robust Lifecycle (Corrected)

App Starts: OnStartUp() runs → Initializes local memory.

App Connects: OnConnectToServer() runs → Registers for NAV_X/NAV_Y. Data flows.

Network Drops: Connection is lost.

App Reconnects: OnConnectToServer() runs again → Re-registers for NAV_X/NAV_Y. Data resumes seamlessly.

Idempotency Note: Ensure your Register() function is idempotent (meaning it's safe to call multiple times). In almost all messaging frameworks, re-registering an already registered variable on a new connection is completely safe and expected.

